  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nMonsoonal moisture continues to work in under anticyclonic flow \nacross the western US. Weak forcing and instability is producing\nslow moving showers and thunderstorms resulting in heavy rain\nacross Wyoming this past evening. These showers and thunderstorms\nare expected to move north and east across the WY/MT border \nthrough the overnight hours. For this afternoon and evening, hi- \nres models are showing more robust showers and thunderstorms with \nconsistent runs initiating showers and thunderstorms over the \nAbsaroka /Beartooths early to mid afternoon. With anomalously high\npwats of 1.2-1.5\" and modest instability (MUCAPE up to 1000 \nJ/kg), thunderstorms will have even better potential to produce \nheavy rainfall. Those recreating in the mountains and foothills, \nespecially near any burn scars, should plan ahead as conditions \ncan change quickly. With instability extending through Thursday \nmorning, showers will are likely to persist across southeast MT. A\nFlood Watch remains in effect for the Absaroka/Beartooth \nmountains and area foothills along with the Bighorns and their \narea foothills 1pm today through 2am Thursday. \n\nWeak subsidence is expected move in late Thursday through \nSaturday, allowing for warmer and drier conditions. Moderate to \nhigh chances for temperatures of at least 100 degrees F exist \nFriday for areas Billings east, with high chances extending to \nmost of the region Saturday. Similar probs as Friday exist for \nSunday. Ensembles are hinting at energy moving through northern \nMT over the weekend which would increase wind potential and thus \nfire weather concerns. As we enter a warmer and drier period, \nthose who missed precipitation over the week should use extra \ncaution as to not cause a spark. TS",
